  "learn": {
   "exam": [
    "You must _describe and compare the structure of a plant cell with an animal cell_. A plant cell has every part an animal cell has, and three more: a cell wall, chloroplasts and a large permanent vacuole.",
    {
     "head": "Cell wall",
     "group": "wall",
     "text": "The cell wall is a strong layer outside the cell membrane, made of cellulose. It supports the cell and keeps its shape, and it stops the cell bursting when water enters it."
    },
    {
     "head": "Cell membrane",
     "group": "membrane",
     "text": "The cell membrane lies just inside the cell wall. It controls the movement of substances into and out of the cell, as in an animal cell. The wall and the membrane are two separate layers."
    },
    {
     "head": "Chloroplasts",
     "group": "chloroplast",
     "text": "Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll, which absorbs light. They are the site of photosynthesis. Only the cells in the green parts of a plant have chloroplasts: a root hair cell has none."
    },
    {
     "head": "Large permanent vacuole",
     "group": "vacuole",
     "text": "The large permanent vacuole is filled with cell sap, a solution of sugars and salts in water. It stores water and helps to support the cell."
    },
    {
     "head": "The parts it shares with an animal cell",
     "group": [
      "nucleus",
      "cytoplasm",
      "mitochondria",
      "ribosomes"
     ],
     "text": "A plant cell also has a nucleus, cytoplasm, mitochondria and ribosomes, with the same functions as in an animal cell. In a plant cell the cytoplasm is a thin layer between the cell membrane and the vacuole."
    },
    {
     "head": "The wall and the membrane separate (Supplement)",
     "text": "Place living plant cells in a strong salt solution and they lose water. The vacuole and the cytoplasm shrink, and the cell membrane separates from the cell wall: so they are two different layers. Press \"After losing water\" in the picture: the same kind of cells in water and after losing water. This is plasmolysis. Why the cells lose water is in 3.2 Osmosis (Supplement)."
    }
   ],
   "golden": [
    "The cell wall and the cell membrane are two different layers, and a plant cell has both: the wall outside, the membrane just inside it. Examiners report candidates who label the wall \"cell membrane\", or who write that the wall controls what enters the cell.",
    "Not every plant cell has chloroplasts. A root hair cell is in the soil, with no light, so it has none. Examiners report candidates who think that root hair cells contain chloroplasts."
   ],
   "examFocus": [
    {
     "tag": "Compare",
     "text": "what both cells have and what only one has: \"Both cells have …; only the plant cell has …\"."
    },
    {
     "tag": "Describe",
     "text": "the function of the cell wall: it supports the cell and stops it bursting."
    }
   ],
   "further": [
    {
     "ref": "3.2 Osmosis (Supplement)",
     "text": "Why a plant cell becomes turgid in water, and why its membrane separates from the wall in a strong salt solution."
    },
    {
     "ref": "6.1 Photosynthesis",
     "text": "What chloroplasts make, and how the plant uses it."
    },
    {
     "ref": "4.1 Biological molecules",
     "text": "Cellulose, like starch, is made from glucose."
    }
   ]
  },
  "keywords": [
   {
    "term": "cell wall",
    "def": "A rigid layer outside the cell membrane of plant, fungal and prokaryotic cells. In plants it is made of cellulose; in fungi and prokaryotes it is not. Animal cells have none."
   },
   {
    "term": "cellulose",
    "def": "A polymer of glucose that forms plant cell walls. Humans have no enzyme that digests it, so it leaves the gut undigested: this is fibre (roughage)."
   },
   {
    "term": "chloroplast",
    "def": "A structure in plant cells (and in some protoctists) that contains chlorophyll and carries out photosynthesis."
   },
   {
    "term": "vacuole",
    "def": "A space in a cell, filled with fluid and surrounded by a membrane. A plant cell has one large permanent vacuole, filled with cell sap: water entering it makes the cell turgid, and water leaving it makes the cell flaccid. Some animal cells have small, temporary vacuoles."
   },
   {
    "term": "chlorophyll",
    "def": "A green pigment, found in chloroplasts, that transfers energy from light into energy in chemicals, for the synthesis of carbohydrates."
   }
  ],
